MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Generally, plyr drops row names from data frames (because I don't
think they're a good idea), but keeps them for arrays (because they
have subtly different semantics which somehow do seem ok to me)

Hadley

On Wed, May 28, 2014 at 4:48 PM, Stavros Macrakis (Σταῦρος Μακράκης)
<macr...@alum.mit.edu> wrote:
> a_ply preserves original row names, while d_ply starts each group from 1.
>
> I'd think that either both should preserve them, or neither should. Am I
> missing something?
>
>           -s
>
>> df <- data.frame(a=c(1,2,1),b=c(2,2,2))
>> df
>   a b
> 1 1 2
> 2 2 2
> 3 1 2
>
>> a_ply(df,1,dput)
> structure(list(a = 1, b = 2), .Names = c("a", "b"), row.names = 1L, class =
> "data.frame")
> structure(list(a = 2, b = 2), .Names = c("a", "b"), row.names = 2L, class =
> "data.frame")
> structure(list(a = 1, b = 2), .Names = c("a", "b"), row.names = 3L, class =
> "data.frame")
>> dput(llply(alply(t2,1),row.names),,NULL)
> list(`1` = "1", `2` = "2", `3` = "3")
>
>> d_ply(df,.(a),dput)
> structure(list(a = c(1, 1), b = c(2, 2)), .Names = c("a", "b"), class =
> "data.frame", row.names = c(NA, -2L), vars = "a")
> structure(list(a = 2, b = 2), .Names = c("a", "b"), class = "data.frame",
> row.names = c(NA, -1L), vars = "a")
>> dput(llply(dlply(t2,.(a)),row.names),,NULL)
> list(`1` = c("1", "2"), `2` = "1")
>

